I know I've seen this correlation pointed out before, some years ago --
likely in National Review or Human Events, or maybe someplace even
less mainstream.  I think to most Americans the suggestion that there
might be any significance to the date sounds really stupid, as Lefty
suggests.  I know that to many people of Krzys's background, the
significance of something like that seems self-evident.  If a Communist
government or organization sets up a celebration for international
propaganda purposes, it will not accidently set it on a date that
has other and incompatible ideological significance.  (Somebody
would notice, sooner or later, and somebody else's ass would be
grass, as they say).  This kind of symbolism is taken very seriously
in communist circles, but mostly not in the US.  Krzys' asks Lefty
(wasn't it?) if he would be willing to participate in a celebration
that fell on Hitler's birthday; I think that most Americans would be
inclined to shrug that off as an unfortunate but irrelevant coincidence
unless they were presented with evidence that the date had been
deliberately chosen for that reason. 
    So, if the date for Earth Day was chosen by someone thinking like
a (naive) American, undoubtedly the coincidence is just coincidence.
If it was chosen by someone thinking like a communist, it's very
likely not coincidence.  Of course, even the farthest-left environmentalist
would be showing singular stupidity and ignorance picking a date
for Earth Day that celebrated old "Communism = Socialism + electrification"
Lenin, but then, as many posters have gently and graciously pointed out,
stupidity and ignorance are not unknown among environmental activists.
    Still, I find it extremely unlikely that the choice was deliberate.
After all, it doesn't even have good symbolic value--how many Americans
know, or care, when Lenin's birthday is?  And as far as I know, the
coincidence has been pointed out only in very right-wing media; if it
meant anything to the ecofreaks, you'd expect them to mention it now
and then.  As somebody pointed out earlier (I think), if the founders of 
Earth Day had wanted to link it to communism, they would probably have set
it on May Day.


