Date: Wed, 28 Apr 93 12:31:48 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: META: Legalisms, Technicalities, and a FAQ

In this post I call for changes in the List rules (I acknowledge that I
have no "right" to, that the List is the property of the ExI) and for a new
approach to generating a FAQ.

The ongoing debate about copyright infringements, court injunctions against
Mr. Singh (for example), formal charges (I'm not referring to this case,
necessarily), and so forth raises in my mind the key question: 

Why does this list rely so much on nitpicky legalisms and less on simpler
systems based on "refusal to do business with" principles, that is, simple
market mechanisms?

The formal list rules seem rarely to stop flaming and such behavior.
Instead, they seem to be like "technicalities" or "speed traps" that some
list members use to their advantage (by filing formal charges against those
who've run afoul of these technicalities).

(I once attempted to satirize this process by using language like "And one
additional warning for failing to use the mandatory "I**2 + UPLOADING"
prefix." This satire didn't go over too well, so I'll instead just make my
point directly and without humor.)

Some suggestions:

  1. Simplify the list rules so everyone knows what they are, without
having to dig up their copy of the list rules (if they still have them).
Avoid multiplying the "mandatory" list prefixes. 

2. Rather than catching disruptive posters in "technicalities," why not
simple ignore them? There would be no debate about Greens as
Marxist-Leninists, to pick just one current example, if people ignored the
comment in the first place, nor would there be acrimonious debate about
metempsychosis and aliens from the future if we all (myself included,
natch) just ignored such idiocy.

Robin Hanson's "Polite Society" was aimed in this direction, but hasn't
really taken off. Generally, those who write foolish posts, and those who
just as foolishly debate them, should have this behavior affect their
"reputations." (And of course we don't need formal mechanisms to impement
this, just common sense.)

3. Serious offenders should just be *kicked off* the list by a simple
*majority* vote of the ExI board (or two thirds, or whatever). No searching
for "technicalities" would be needed. 

Note: I notice that apparently only a few ExI Board members are active on
this list, or are even subscribed to it. This makes it tough for the Board
to police the List. (And placing the duty solely on Harry Shapiro seems
both unwise and overly burdensome.)

(I'm not sure who the Board is these days--I can't seem to find it listed
in "Extropy" #10--but people like Dave Krieger, Mark Plus, Simon! Levy,
Russell Whitaker, J. Storrs Hall, and Michael Perry, who I think of as
being the Board, are not very active on the List. Perhaps a new kind of
Board, associated with the List more closely, is needed. (I'm not
volunteering and will not serve, even if asked. So there's no conflict of
interest here.)

4. I would urge newcomers to the List to spend at least a few weeks simply
*reading* the List before they begin posting. (Someday the List software
may even allow "read only" access for some period.) This accomplishes
several things. First, they become acquainted with the personalities and so
are (somewhat) less likely to begin flaming at the drop of a hat. Second,
they get a feel for the nature of the debate, the topics that are
considered suitable, the topics that have already been beaten to death,
etc. Third, they're more likely to have something *new* to say.

Failing this, old-timers should try to just avoid responding to "newbie
bait." As others have noted, the List seems to be nearly always in the
thrall of some newcomer who wants to debate the basics. Sadly, people
respond, and often with irritation that only makes the problem worse.

5. A FAQ is Needed!  This would go a long way toward answering the many
questions that keep coming up.

Last summer, when I raised this issue, I was told by someone to just go
ahead and volunteer for the job. I said I'd only consider it after I'd been
reading the List for a long while, at least 6 months. (How can a newbie
write the FAQ?!)

Well, it's been 10 months since I joined the List and I am willing to
volunteer this time around. I understand that Ray Cromwell intended to
write one, but other projects (rewriting the List software, I gather) have
intervened.

I suggest the matter of a FAQ be reopened.

My proposal would be for me to just *write* the damn thing, with
contributions as I get them (but I'm not holding my breath, as I've seen
too many groups never get a FAQ because the ostensible coordinator was
waiting for "stone soup" contributions which never quite came in. This is
happening even now on the Cypherpunks group, which at least three people
have volunteered, then let it slide.) A rough draft would at least be a
target for people to criticize and add to.

Another solution, more radical and yet more in line with the politics of
this group, is for any of us who wish to write a FAQ to just go ahead and
do so, with or without the blessing of the ExI. (To avoid "copyright"
infringements, authors could use "Xtrpns" or "Fubars" as a stand-in.) Then
the "market" could decide, with people requesting whichever FAQ they
wanted.

Feedback is welcome.

-Tim May
