From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: CASH: Disney $Bobbins$ ???

Back when I was married, my ex-spouse was in a baby-sitting co-operative.
It wasn't pure barter (a two party transaction), but it was close:

If you babysat another member's children, you got an hour's credit
per child per clock hour.  If someone else watched your children,
then you lost the same amount.  Both parents called in to the
tally keeper to report sittings (thus cross checking).  You could
go into debt a maximum of 10 hours, after which you were no longer
eligible to get your children watched.  There was no limit on
credit you could accumulate.  The credit/debit standings were
distributed periodically, and you were supposed to call the most
negative person in hours first, then work up the list until you
found someone who could sit for the time you needed.  This mechanism
acted to return people to zero hours.  Of course, in this way
the whole system was a zero sum operation.  No currency changed hands,
and of course no income was declared.

I would imagine that a larger cooperative, skirting closer to the
edge as far as getting into trouble with the IRS, could use a
labor-hour standard.  The base unit could be an hour of unskilled
labor (like helping someone move furniture or mow the lawn).  
Skilled labor could be priced at higher rates (eg a doctor could
price an office visit at 10 unskilled labor hours).  Some means
could be used to create a positive-sum result.  For example, a
person could convert materials owned by the cooperative into a
house owned by the cooperative, and get a credit without there 
being a corresponding debit for anyone else.

